
1.1.1 JDK — Java Development Kit
Your syllabus covers JDK components, javac, java, jar, javadoc, diagnostic tools, JAVA_HOME, PATH, multiple JDK versions, distributions, OpenJDK and jlink. �
Module_1_1_Java_Platform_Fundamentals_Deep_Mastery.md
Basic
What is the JDK?
Why is the JDK required for Java development?
What are the major components of a JDK?
What is javac?
What is the java launcher?
What is jar?
What is javadoc?
What is jdb?
What is jcmd?
What is jstack?
What is jmap?
What is jps?
What is jstat?
What is JFR?
What is jlink?
What is jpackage?
What is JAVA_HOME?
What is the purpose of PATH?
Intermediate
What is the difference between JDK, JRE and JVM?
Does the JDK contain a JVM?
Which JDK component actually compiles source code?
Which JDK tools are useful for diagnosing a running JVM?
When would you use jstack instead of jmap?
When would you use jcmd?
What problem does JFR solve?
Why might you need multiple JDK versions on one machine?
How does JAVA_HOME affect Maven or Gradle?
What happens if JAVA_HOME points to the wrong JDK?
Why can java -version and javac -version report different versions?
Why is a JDK distribution different from the Java language itself?
Advanced
What happens internally when javac compiles a Java source file?
How does the compiler transform Java source into bytecode?
Which parts of the JDK are required only during development?
Why can a production container potentially use a smaller runtime image?
What is a custom runtime image?
How does jlink create a custom runtime?
What are the advantages and disadvantages of using a minimal runtime image?
What is the relationship between OpenJDK and commercial JDK distributions?
Can two JDK vendors behave differently while remaining Java-compatible?
What factors would you consider when selecting a JDK distribution for production?
Conceptual
If the JVM executes bytecode, why does the JDK need javac?
Can you run an already compiled Java application without installing development tools?
Why isn't "JDK = JVM + compiler" a complete description?
If two servers use different JDK vendors, what compatibility assumptions can you safely make?
Why shouldn't developers blindly assume that every JDK tool is appropriate to run continuously in production?
Implementation
Install two JDK versions and switch between them.
Compile a Java program using javac.
Run it using the java launcher.
Package it into a JAR.
Generate Javadocs.
Use jps to identify a Java process.
Use jstack to obtain a thread dump.
Use jcmd against a running JVM.
Capture a JFR recording.
Create a custom runtime using jlink.
Major Traps
JDK ≠ JVM.
JRE is not the same thing as JDK.
JAVA_HOME and PATH can point to different Java installations.
javac version and runtime version compatibility matters.
JDK distribution ≠ Java specification.
A JDK is not required merely to execute an already compiled application.
Modern Java distributions no longer revolve around the old standalone JRE installation model. �
Module_1_1_Java_Platform_Fundamentals_Deep_Mastery.md
Production / Design
Your Spring Boot service runs in Kubernetes. Would you install a full JDK in the production container? Why or why not?
Your company has Java 17 and Java 21 applications. How would you manage both?
A production JVM is consuming excessive memory. Which JDK diagnostic tools would you consider?
How would you standardize JDK versions across development, CI and production?
What risks arise when developers and production use different JDK vendors or versions?
