1.1.6 Bytecode
Your syllabus specifically expects understanding bytecode's role in interpretation/JIT and using bytecode inspection to understand runtime behavior. �
Module_1_1_Java_Platform_Fundamentals_Deep_Mastery.md
Basic
What is Java bytecode?
Where is bytecode stored?
What extension normally contains bytecode?
Who generates bytecode?
Who executes bytecode?
Intermediate
Why does Java use bytecode?
What are bytecode instructions?
What is a bytecode operand stack?
What are local variables in JVM bytecode?
What is a method descriptor?
What does invokestatic mean?
What does invokevirtual mean?
What does invokespecial mean?
What does getfield do?
What does putfield do?
Advanced
How does bytecode differ from native machine code?
How does the JVM verify bytecode?
How does bytecode feed the interpreter?
How does JIT compilation transform frequently executed bytecode?
How can bytecode inspection explain overloaded versus overridden methods?
How can bytecode reveal compiler-generated constructs?
How can bytecode help debug an unexpected Java-language behavior?
Implementation
Compile:
class Test {
    int add(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;
    }
}
Then inspect it using:
javap -c -p Test
Identify the instructions for:
local variable loading
arithmetic
method invocation
return
Compare bytecode generated for:
static method
instance method
constructor
Major Trap
Bytecode is not "machine code for the JVM".
Be able to explain why JVM bytecode is an intermediate instruction set executed by a JVM implementation.
